    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 132018\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n118 PM MST Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Lingering rain chances mainly across Arizona high terrain this\n  afternoon before ending completely this evening.\n  \n- Below normal temperatures are forecast through Wednesday before\n  creeping back into the normal range late week. \n\n- Breezy to windy conditions will impact the western deserts\n  through Thursday before spreading across the rest of southern\n  and central Arizona by Friday. Advisory level winds will be\n  possible by Friday, particularly across southeast Arizona.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\n\nScattered light to moderate rain showers were continuing to fall\nthis morning across south-central AZ and a lot of the area was\nlocked under thick low-level clouds. Soundings from the Phoenix\narea this morning showed a nearly fully saturated layer from the\nsurface to 700mb. Above 700mb there is a lot of dry air. This\nmoisture has been trapped below 700mb for the past couple days and\nthe persistent S-SW flow has continued to support modest upslope\nascent for showers to continuously develop. Most hi-res models\nhave showers shifting east of the Phoenix area for the afternoon\nand evening, focusing over the higher terrain areas, but an\nisolated shower still cannot be ruled out across Phoenix through\n4-6 PM this evening. Heading into this evening and tonight the\nlayer below 700mb will finally dry out as a dry cold front moves\nthrough, associated with a low pressure system that will pass\nthrough northern AZ (currently over southern NV). The rain and \nclouds have stuck around a bit longer than initially thought and \nas a result temperatures are a bit cooler. Temperatures in the \nPhoenix area at noon were still in the mid and upper 60s across \neastern areas to low 70s across western areas. Most of Phoenix \nwill likely fail to reach 80 degrees this afternoon, especially \nthe longer the clouds can stick around.\n\nAs mentioned, a dry front will sweep through the area this evening\nand tonight, which eliminate shower chances across the entire\nlocal area by midnight. This front and the passing low pressure\nsystem will also bring some cooler air that will be most\nnoticeablewith the temperatures the next couple mornings. Low \ntemperatures tomorrow morning are forecast to be in the 50s across\nmost lower desert areas with some low-lying exposed areas and \nsome rural areas dipping into the 40s. Some localized valley fog \ncannot be fully ruled out tomorrow morning following the wet \nperiod, better potential in sheltered valleys NE and E of Phoenix,\nbut there will be a lot of dry air entrainment above the surface.\nSkies will be clear Wednesday and temperatures will warm into the\n70s across south-central AZ and reach 78-83 degrees across some \nwestern desert locations. With strengthening surface pressure to \nthe north behind the upper level low pressure system, breezy to \nlocally windy northerlies (25-35 mph wind gusts) will develop \nacross southeast California and southwest Arizona from this \nafternoon through tomorrow.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nForecast confidence is increasing for advisory level winds\ndeveloping later this week across eastern and southern Arizona.\nGuidance continues to show a fast moving dry shortwave trough\ndiving southwestward into the western part of the large scale\ntrough lingering across the Southwestern U.S. Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. This disturbance is then expected to form a cut- \noff low just to our southwest at the same time strong ridging \nedges into California and the Great Basin. \n\nThursday looks to be a transition day with a continuation of\nenhanced northerly winds down the Lower CO River Valley while\ntemperatures warm closer to normals as northerly downslope \nwarming spreads through much of Arizona. These northerly winds\nshould quickly shift out of the east late Thursday night/Friday \nmorning as the cut-off low forms and drifts southwestward off the \ncoast of Baja. A strong pressure gradient will then develop across\nArizona in response to strong surface high pressure building over\nthe southern Rockies and the Plains to the east and low pressure \nto our west. There may even be a distinguishable backdoor cold \nfront boundary that will move through Friday morning. Guidance \nshows 30-45 kt H8 winds setting up across southern Arizona Friday \nmorning which should translate to widespread 30-40 mph easterly gusts\nat times to as high as 45 mph across portions of Pinal and \nsouthern Maricopa Counties. A Wind Advisory is expected to be \nneeded for these areas on Friday and possibly into Saturday \nmorning before the gradient begins to relax.\n\nThe strong easterly winds on Friday will also usher in some cooler\nair from New Mexico, keeping daytime highs in the low to mid 80s\nacross the Phoenix area. The western lower deserts will be less\naffected by this back-door front with highs closer to 90 degrees.\n\nModel uncertainty increases for the weekend as there is some \nquestion of how long the cut-off low will sit just to our south \nbefore ejecting back to the east and whether or not the remnants \nof TC Simon will play any role in our weather. As of right now, \nguidance shows the cut-off weakening and shifting east northeast \nacross northern Mexico later Saturday into Sunday while the \nremnants of Simon mostly likely bypasses our region to the \nsoutheast. Before this occurs, the positioning of the cut-off low\nand the increased easterly flow is expected to bring good mid and\nupper level moisture advection into Arizona and southern \nCalifornia on Friday, but likely little if any low level moisture.\nThe most likely scenario calls for increased higher level clouds \non Saturday and a 10-20% chance of showers focused more toward \nsoutheast Arizona. However, there are some ensemble members (maybe\n20%) that try to pull better low level moisture (even potentially\nfrom Simon's remnants) into at least southeast Arizona Saturday \ninto early Sunday. If this were to occur, it would bring much \nbetter rain chances as far northwest as the Phoenix area. \nAlthough still unlikely, it will be something to watch over the \nnext few days. By early next week, we should have another ridge \nwith dry air advection moving in from the west leading to quieter \nweather conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1815Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nThe primary aviation weather concern will continue to be lingering\nshowers with low CIGs through this afternoon. Showers will\ncontinue into this afternoon, resulting in lowered CIGs down to\naround 1-3 kft, along with brief MVFR visibilities, at times \nprimarily at KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT. Expect some improvement in \nCIGs with SCT-BKN bases generally aoa 3-5 kft this afternoon \nbefore clearing out this evening. Westerly winds will increase \ninto this afternoon with a few gusts around 15-20 kts. Winds \nweaken this evening before becoming light and variable tonight \ninto the overnight period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns are anticipated through the\nnext 24 hours under mostly clear skies. Winds will favor the W to\nNW at KIPL, while favoring the N to NW at KBLH. A few gusts to\naround 20 kts are expected at KBLH, tapering off this evening.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIsolated showers will persist across the eastern districts, mainly\nhigh terrain areas, through this afternoon before drier air \nsweeps through the area behind a passing cold front this evening\nand tonight. Elevated moisture levels will keep MinRHs today \nbetween 25-35% for the western districts to 40-70% for the eastern\ndistricts before lowering down to 20-40% Wednesday. Northerly \nbreezy winds will develop across southeast CA and southwest AZ \ntoday and strengthen each day through Thursday before strong \neasterly winds spread through the region by Friday. Below normal \ntemperatures through Wednesday will eventually return to near \nnormal temperatures late week. Some moisture return Friday into \nthe weekend may bring low- end rain chances by Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Smith \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
